<h1>Prerequisites</h1>
<p>Orcraft needs Python on each machine and one network port for remote endpoints. Everything else
  is installed automatically by pip.</p>
<p>Check <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/system-reference/#operating-systems">supported operating
  systems</a> for platform coverage and validated environments.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th class="docs-table__number" scope="col">#</th>
      <th>Step</th>
      <th>Where</th>
      <th>What to do</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td class="docs-table__number">1</td>
      <td>Install Python 3.14+</td>
      <td>Controller and endpoints</td>
      <td>One installer from python.org</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="docs-table__number">2</td>
      <td>Create a virtual environment</td>
      <td>Controller and endpoints</td>
      <td>Three commands below</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="docs-table__number">3</td>
      <td>Allow endpoint traffic</td>
      <td>Remote endpoints only</td>
      <td>One TCP port and ping</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Running tests only on the controller? Skip the endpoint traffic setup.</p>
<p>The controller needs a desktop session for its GUI. Endpoints have no GUI, so they can run
  headless - on a server or over SSH.</p>

<h2 id="python">Install Python</h2>
<p>Install Python 3.14 or later on the controller and every endpoint. Download it from
  <a class="text-accent" href="https://www.python.org/downloads/">python.org</a>; on Windows,
  select <strong>Add python.exe to PATH</strong> in the installer.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>Verify</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("python --version") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux, macOS</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("python3 --version") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="virtual-environment">Create a virtual environment</h2>
<p>This keeps Orcraft isolated from the system Python and avoids Linux's
  <code>externally-managed-environment</code> error.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Step</th>
      <th>Windows</th>
      <th>Linux / macOS</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Create</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("python -m venv .venv") }}</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("python3 -m venv .venv") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Activate</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell(".venv\\Scripts\\activate") }}</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("source .venv/bin/activate") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Install</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip install orcraft") }}</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("pip install orcraft") }}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Activate the environment whenever you open a new terminal before running Orcraft.</p>
<p>If PowerShell blocks the activation script, allow it for the current terminal only - no
  administrator rights or machine-wide change:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned") }}

<h2 id="remote-endpoints">Allow remote endpoint traffic</h2>
<p>Skip this section for local-only tests. For each remote endpoint, allow the controller to reach
  the port the endpoint listens on and allow ping between the two machines.</p>

<h3 id="tcp-port">TCP port</h3>
<p>Endpoints listen on TCP <strong>50051</strong> unless you choose another port, so the commands
  below use that default:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Endpoint OS</th>
      <th>What to do</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName 'Orcraft Endpoint' -Direction Inbound -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 50051 -Action Allow") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux with ufw</td>
      <td>{{ cmd.copyCell("sudo ufw allow 50051/tcp") }}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>Allow incoming connections when macOS prompts the first time the endpoint runs</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Running the endpoint on a different port? Select it with <code>-port</code>, use that number in
  the rule, and enter the same port when adding the endpoint in the controller. Using a different
  firewall? Add the equivalent inbound TCP rule. The controller needs no inbound rule.</p>

<h3 id="icmp-ping">ICMP (Ping)</h3>
<p>The controller pings each endpoint to confirm it is reachable before connecting to it. Lab and
  office networks usually allow this already; if an endpoint appears Offline, allow ICMP echo
  requests in both directions:</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>OS</th>
      <th>What to allow</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Windows</td>
      <td>Inbound "File and Printer Sharing (Echo Request - ICMPv4/ICMPv6)"</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linux</td>
      <td>ICMP in <code>ufw</code>, <code>firewalld</code>, <code>nftables</code> or
        <code>iptables</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>macOS</td>
      <td>Incoming ping in the firewall; "Block all incoming connections" must be off</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

